2D Plate Tectonics Simulator — Build Spec
Sep 20, 2026 · @Wade
Scope and design philosophy
Build a 2D plate tectonics simulator on a torus (flat map, wraparound in both x and y). The goal is a map that evolves over hundreds of millions of years and produces recognizable tectonic geography — continents, ocean basins, mid-ocean ridge/transform staircases, fracture zones, trenches with volcanic arcs, collisional orogens, rift valleys — none of which are drawn directly. All of it emerges from plate motion plus boundary rules plus a physical elevation model.
Three non-negotiable principles
1. Crust lives on Lagrangian points, not a grid. The simulation state is a point cloud that moves with the plates. A grid is produced each frame for display only. Storing crust on a fixed grid and advecting it resamples every step and blurs continents into mush within ~50 Myr of simulated time. This is the most common way these projects fail.
2. Elevation is derived, never painted. Height is a pure function of crustal thickness, density, and seafloor age via isostasy and half-space cooling. Nothing in the codebase should assign an elevation directly. Get this right and mountain roots, shelf breaks, ridge topography, and post-orogenic rebound all appear for free.
3. Plates must be able to break and merge. A simulator that only moves a fixed set of plates produces a world frozen in one configuration, dead within a few hundred Myr. Rifting (splitting a plate) and suturing (welding two on continental collision) are what generate Wilson cycles. Core requirements, not polish.
Explicitly out of scope for v1
• Erosion and sedimentation. Deliberately deferred. Do not add a stream-power or thermal erosion pass. Design the elevation model so erosion can later be added as a mass-transfer step feeding back into isostasy, but do not implement it now.
• Spherical geometry, map projections, Euler poles. The domain is a flat torus.
• Climate, hydrology, rivers, biomes.
• Mantle convection. Plate motions are prescribed and perturbed heuristically, not solved for.
• Second-order ridge segmentation (overlapping spreading centers, non-transform offsets at tens-of-km scale). First-order ridge/transform segmentation only.
Target scale
Domain roughly 40,000 × 20,000 km. Point spacing 25–50 km, giving on the order of 50k–200k crust points. Timestep 1 Myr. Should run interactively in a browser on CPU; structure the hot loops (neighbor search, splatting) so they could move to GPU later without restructuring the data model.
Data model
Use structure-of-arrays (typed arrays), not arrays of objects. Point counts are large and the hot loops are numeric.
