Torus mechanics
The domain wraps in both axes. Every distance and every difference vector must go through a wrap function. No exceptions. A single unwrapped dx in the neighbor search produces phantom interactions across the map that are painful to diagnose, because the simulation keeps running and quietly produces wrong geography.
function wrapDelta(d, L) {
  if (d >  L * 0.5) d -= L;
  if (d < -L * 0.5) d += L;
  return d;
}
function wrapPos(p, L) {
  p %= L;
  return p < 0 ? p + L : p;
}
function dist2(ax, ay, bx, by) {
  const dx = wrapDelta(bx - ax, W), dy = wrapDelta(by - ay, H);
  return dx*dx + dy*dy;
}
Enforce this structurally: never subtract raw coordinates anywhere in the codebase. Route everything through wrapDelta.
Circular means
A plain arithmetic average of coordinates is wrong on a torus. A plate straddling the seam gets a centroid on the opposite side of the map, which then corrupts its rotation. Use the circular mean per axis: map each coordinate to an angle, average the unit vectors, map back.
function circularMean(values, L) {
  let sx = 0, sy = 0;
  for (const v of values) {
    const a = (v / L) * 2 * Math.PI;
    sx += Math.cos(a); sy += Math.sin(a);
  }
  let a = Math.atan2(sy, sx);
  if (a < 0) a += 2 * Math.PI;
  return (a / (2 * Math.PI)) * L;
}
This degenerates when a plate wraps the full width — the resultant vector goes to zero and the centroid is undefined. Detect low resultant magnitude and fall back to the previous step's centroid.
Spatial hash
Uniform grid with cell size approximately the interaction radius. Cell index wraps with modulo; the 3x3 neighborhood query wraps too. Rebuild each step — points move far enough that incremental updates are not worth the complexity.
Delaunay on a torus
Simplest correct approach: replicate points within one interaction radius of each edge into ghost copies on the opposite side, triangulate the padded set with a standard library, then map ghost triangles back to their originals and de-duplicate. Padding of two to three cell widths is plenty. A true periodic Delaunay implementation is more elegant but not worth writing.
Rendering the seam
When drawing, tile the canvas 3x3 (or at minimum draw wrapped duplicates of anything crossing an edge) so features spanning the seam appear continuous. A user panning across the seam should see no discontinuity — if they do, something in the physics is probably also wrong.
Plate motion
Each plate carries linear velocity plus angular velocity about its own centroid:
vx_point = plate.vx - plate.omega * wrapDelta(y - plate.cy, H);
vy_point = plate.vy + plate.omega * wrapDelta(x - plate.cx, W);
Do not skip the rotation term. Without it every boundary is one type along its entire length and the map looks like a textbook cartoon. With rotation, a single boundary naturally grades from spreading at one end, through transform, to convergent at the other — which is what real boundaries do, and it is most of what makes the fault map look plausible.
