Advection
Forward Euler is fine at 1 Myr steps — velocities are constant within a step and there is no stiff coupling. Advect, then wrapPos. Advect slab points too, scaled by their coupling.
Where velocities come from
Not solved from mantle convection (out of scope). Instead:
• Initialize each plate with a random velocity in range.
• Each step, apply a small random walk to vx, vy, omega so configurations drift rather than freeze.
• Apply slab pull: a plate with a lot of attached subducting slab accelerates toward its trenches. Sum over slab points with high coupling, weighted by age_at_sub (older = denser = stronger pull).
• Apply ridge push, much weaker: a mild force away from spreading centers.
• Damp toward zero net momentum across all plates so the whole system does not drift.
Slab pull is worth the effort. It is the dominant driver on the real Earth, and it makes the sim self-organizing rather than purely random — plates with long subduction zones speed up, which closes the loop between boundary processes and plate kinematics.
Boundary extraction and classification
Do not use the naive method
The tempting approach — flag every point whose neighbor has a different plate — produces a fuzzy band two points wide. It is not a line, it cannot be rendered cleanly, and it gives you no object to attach history to. Reject it.
Use the Voronoi dual
A boundary is the set of Voronoi edges separating points of different plates. Since the Delaunay triangulation is already being built, this is nearly free: for each Delaunay edge whose two endpoints belong to different plates, emit the dual Voronoi edge (the segment joining the circumcenters of the two adjacent triangles).
The result is a genuine polyline network — one edge wide, connected, geometrically clean. Walk connected components to get faults as objects with length, endpoints, and identity.
Stable fault IDs
Rebuild the network each step, then match to the previous step's faults by overlap of (plate_a, plate_b) and spatial proximity of endpoints. Carry IDs forward. Without this, nothing downstream can have history.
Classify by relative velocity
For each boundary edge, evaluate at its midpoint. Include each plate's rotation term when computing velocities.
const vRel = velAt(plateB, mx, my) - velAt(plateA, mx, my);  // vector
const n = edgeNormal;   // unit, pointing from A into B
const t = edgeTangent;  // unit, perpendicular to n
const vn = dot(vRel, n);   // positive = opening, negative = closing
const vt = dot(vRel, t);   // shear
const obliquity = Math.atan2(Math.abs(vt), -vn);
const rate = Math.hypot(vn, vt);
Store the continuous obliquity angle. Do not bucket into three types. 0 = pure convergence, π/2 = pure transform, π = pure divergence, and everything real lives in between. Transpressional and transtensional boundaries are common and you lose them the moment you snap to categories.
Sub-classify by what is on each side
The obliquity gives the kinematics; the crust types on either side give the feature. Use continental fraction averaged over the points adjacent to the edge.
