Ridge segmentation — the orthogonal staircase
This is the section most likely to be implemented wrong. Read it carefully.
Raw Voronoi boundary polylines will not produce the ridge/transform staircase on their own. They are jagged at the point-spacing scale, which is noise, not structure — wrong scale, wrong character, and no memory, because the jaggedness re-randomizes every step. The staircase has to be constructed explicitly.
Why the staircase exists
A spreading center can only do one thing: create crust symmetrically, perpendicular to the spreading direction. If the overall boundary trend is oblique to the spreading direction, no single continuous ridge can accommodate that motion. So the boundary decomposes into pure-mode pieces:
• Ridge segments perpendicular to the spreading direction (pure opening)
• Transform faults parallel to the spreading direction (pure strike-slip, nothing created or destroyed)
The orthogonal staircase is the only decomposition in which every piece operates in a pure mode. This is good news algorithmically: the geometry is fully determined by spreading direction plus a segmentation length. You are not simulating it, you are constructing it.
Construction
1. Extract the raw boundary polyline from the Voronoi dual (previous section). This is your topology.
2. Rotate into the spreading frame: let x̂ = spreading direction, ŷ = perpendicular. In this frame, ridge segments are vertical and transforms are horizontal.
3. Staircase-approximate the boundary trace in that frame: vertical runs of length L_seg, horizontal jogs to track the original trace. Structurally this is Bresenham. The more oblique the boundary over a stretch, the longer the jogs come out — which is correct, and is exactly how real transform offsets scale with obliquity.
4. Rotate back. Store as RidgeSegment objects with stable IDs.
