Persist the segments
Let segments evolve slowly — lengthen, shorten, propagate, occasionally die or split — but never re-derive from scratch each step. Persistence is the entire point. A transform that exists for only one timestep leaves no trace, and the fracture-zone machinery in a later section depends on segments having continuous identity.
Same algorithm for continental rifts
Oblique continental rifting performs the identical decomposition: rift segments linked by transfer zones (also called accommodation zones). Same geometry, different vocabulary. Write the routine once and parameterize by crust type. The East African Rift is the reference for what the output should look like.
Boundary process rules
Applied each step after classification. These are the rules that turn kinematics into geology.
Divergent — accretion
Where plates separate, a gap opens in the point cloud. Detect gaps as regions where the local point density has dropped below threshold, or more simply by walking each active ridge segment and seeding along it.
Seed new crust points on both sides of the spreading axis, symmetrically:
• continental = 0
• thickness = 7 km
• age = 0
• segment = the ID of the ridge segment that created it — stamp this, it drives fracture zones
• weakness = low
Ridge topography is not drawn. It emerges from the age-depth relation in the elevation model.
Convergent — subduction
Where point clouds from two plates overlap, decide who goes down:
