rule
ocean vs continent
ocean subducts, always
ocean vs ocean
the older crust subducts (colder, denser)
continent vs continent
neither — see collision below
For each subducting point:
1. Remove it from the crust layer.
2. Create a SlabPoint at that location with depth = 0, age_at_sub = its crust age, coupling = 1. Do not simply delete it — the slab layer is a core feature.
3. Transfer a fraction of its thickness to the nearest overriding-plate points as an accretionary wedge. Falls off with distance from the trench.
4. Raise weakness on the overriding points.
Volcanic arc
Place an arc on the overriding plate at a distance inland from the trench determined by slab dip — where the slab reaches roughly 100 km depth. For a typical ~45° dip this is ~150 km inland; for a shallow slab it can be many hundreds. Query the slab layer for this rather than hardcoding a distance — that is what makes flat-slab arc migration work.
Arc effect: add thickness, bump continental fraction upward (arcs manufacture continental crust), raise weakness.
Continental collision — suture
When two continental blocks converge, neither subducts. Instead:
1. Thicken crust on both sides of the boundary, falling off with distance. Peak thickness up to ~70 km.
2. Raise weakness strongly along the collision zone.
3. Merge the two plates into one. Reassign plate on all points of the smaller plate, retire its ID, recompute centroid and velocity from momentum-weighted averages.
Step 3 is mandatory. Suturing is half of what generates supercontinent assembly.
Transform
No crust created or destroyed. Two effects worth having:
• Raise weakness along the fault trace.
• Apply small local transpression or transtension where the fault is not perfectly parallel to the relative motion — slight thickening at restraining bends, thinning at releasing bends. This is what produces pull-apart basins and small pop-up ranges, and it is cheap.
The slab layer
Subducted crust persists as a second point set. This is what lets plate material exist beneath other plates, and it is where the most interesting emergent behavior comes from.
Per-step slab update
For each slab point:
depth += sinkRate(age_at_sub, continental) * dt
dip   += dipEvolution(...)
x, y  += parentPlateVelocity * coupling * dt
coupling *= decay          // weaker grip as it descends
retire when depth > 660 km
Older, colder crust sinks faster and steeper. Buoyant continental material resists sinking — if continental is high, sink rate drops sharply, which is the mechanism behind collision-zone slab behavior.
Querying from the crust layer
For each crust point, spatial-hash lookup into the slab set for slabs beneath it. Retrieve slab_depth, d(depth)/dt, and local slab density. The feedback rules then write themselves — and they give you the two behaviors that matter most.
Flat-slab subduction
Trigger: young buoyant downgoing crust, fast convergence, or a subducted ridge or oceanic plateau.
Effect: dip flattens; the slab scrapes along the base of the overriding plate for hundreds of km inland; arc volcanism shuts off at the normal arc position and migrates far inland; compression is transmitted well away from the trench, producing thick-skinned basement uplift.
This is the Laramide orogeny. If the sim produces it, the slab layer is working.
Slab rollback
Trigger: slab sinks faster than the plates converge, so the trench hinge retreats oceanward.
Effect: the overriding plate goes into extension. Thin the crust there; Airy isostasy drops the elevation with no special-casing, producing basins and ranges, metamorphic core complexes, and back-arc spreading.
Flat slab and rollback are the two primary validation targets for this layer — both are checkable against well-documented real-world examples rather than against a paper figure.
Slab windows
When a spreading ridge is subducted, or a slab detaches (breakoff), leave a gap in the slab field. Where a crust point queries the slab layer and finds a hole where there should be slab, apply: rapid uplift plus anomalous volcanism in a place with no arc.
A few lines once the layer exists, and it produces geological weirdness that looks motivated rather than noisy.
Stretching applied upward
Generalizing the rollback case: the slab layer can apply stress to the crustal plates above it — compression where the slab is strongly coupled and shallow, extension where it is rolling back or absent. Express this as a thickness modification on the overriding crust points, then let isostasy convert it to topography. Do not modify elevation directly.
Elevation model
Two formulas. Together they account for essentially all first-order topography.
Airy isostasy — anything with continental character
const RHO_CRUST_CONT = 2700;   // kg/m^3
const RHO_CRUST_OCEAN = 2900;
const RHO_MANTLE = 3300;
const DATUM = 6.0;             // km, tuned so sea level lands sensibly

const rho = mix(RHO_CRUST_OCEAN, RHO_CRUST_CONT, continental);
const elev = thickness * (1 - rho / RHO_MANTLE) - DATUM;
