Half-space cooling — oceanic crust
function oceanDepth(ageMyr) {
  if (ageMyr < 80) return 2600 + 345 * Math.sqrt(ageMyr);   // meters
  return 5700 - (5700 - 2600 - 345 * Math.sqrt(80)) * 0;    // flattens ~5700 m
}
Ridge crest ~2.6 km deep, 80 Myr crust ~5.7 km, flattening thereafter. Implement the flattening as a smooth asymptote rather than a hard cutoff.
This single function gives you mid-ocean ridge topography, abyssal plains, and the age-depth asymmetry across ridges — none of which need to be drawn.
Blending
For mixed crust, interpolate between the isostatic and cooling results by continental fraction. The transition zone is your continental shelf and slope, so make the blend smooth — a hard switch produces a visible cliff at every coastline.
Sea level
A global constant, but consider making it dynamic: total ocean water is fixed, so when a lot of young (shallow) seafloor exists, ocean basins hold less water and sea level rises, flooding continental margins. This is the mechanism behind the great Cretaceous epicontinental seas. Cheap to add — compute basin volume from the current bathymetry, solve for the level that holds the fixed water volume.
Hard rule
No code path assigns elevation directly. If something needs to be higher, it gets thicker or younger. Grep for direct elevation writes during review; every one is a bug.
Fracture zones and inherited weakness
Two small features with outsized payoff. Both depend on persistence from earlier sections.
Fracture zones fall out for free
Every crust point is stamped with the segment ID that created it. A transform fault is only active between the two offset ridge tips; once crust advects past the tip, both sides belong to the same plate moving together. The fault is dead — but the scar is not.
Across that scar sits crust of two different ages, because it was created at two ridge segments offset in time. The half-space cooling formula already converts age to depth. So the bathymetric step along every fracture zone renders itself, with no additional code.
Those long parallel scars combing across the ocean floor are the most recognizable feature of real seafloor maps, and they emerge purely as a consequence of the segment stamping and the age-depth relation. Nothing needs to be drawn.
To render them explicitly as lines, detect them as loci where the segment ID changes discontinuously between adjacent points and the age difference exceeds a threshold.
Inherited weakness
Give each crust point a weakness scalar:
• Raised wherever deformation happens: collision zones, rift axes, transform traces, arcs, fracture zones.
• Decays slowly — a half-life on the order of 200 Myr.
• Biases rift nucleation: when choosing where to split a plate, strongly prefer high-weakness lines.
The consequence: new rifts preferentially reopen old sutures. This is why the Atlantic opened roughly along the Appalachian–Caledonian suture, and why old orogenic belts keep getting reactivated.
About twenty lines of code. It is the difference between map histories that feel like they have memory and ones that look randomly regenerated each cycle. Implement it — it is one of the highest value-to-effort features in the whole spec.
Plate reorganization
Without this the world is dead after a few hundred Myr. Treat it as core, not polish.
Rifting — splitting a plate
When to trigger. Score each plate each step for rift likelihood:
• Large continental area (big plates insulate the mantle beneath them and go into extension)
• Time since last reorganization
• Net tensile stress from boundary conditions — divergent or rolling-back boundaries on opposite sides
• Presence of high-weakness lineaments crossing the plate
Where to put the rift. Trace a path across the plate that preferentially follows high-weakness points. A least-cost path with cost inversely proportional to weakness works well, and it is what makes rifts reopen old sutures rather than cutting randomly across cratons.
What happens.
1. Assign points on each side of the path to a new plate ID.
2. Give the two plates diverging velocities perpendicular to the rift axis, plus a small differential rotation so the rift opens like a zipper rather than uniformly. Real rifts propagate.
3. Thin the crust along the axis — this immediately drops elevation via isostasy, producing a rift valley before any ocean exists.
4. Once thinning passes a threshold, begin seafloor spreading: create a ridge segment set along the axis using the staircase construction and start accreting oceanic crust.
Stages 3 and 4 give you the East African Rift → Red Sea → Atlantic progression as a continuous process rather than as discrete states.
Suturing — merging plates
Covered in the boundary rules, restated here because it is the counterpart: when continental crust collides with continental crust, weld the plates into one. Reassign point ownership, retire the absorbed plate ID, recompute centroid and velocity.
The Wilson cycle
Rifting plus suturing plus weakness inheritance produce supercontinent assembly and dispersal without any code that knows what a supercontinent is. Expect a cycle length on the order of 300–500 Myr if the tuning is reasonable.
This is the primary whole-system validation target. If you run 2 Gyr and see continents repeatedly gather and disperse, with new oceans opening along old collision belts, the simulation is working. If you see a permanent blob or permanently scattered fragments, the rift/suture balance needs tuning — usually the rift trigger threshold or the weakness decay rate.
Rasterization and rendering
Splat, do not nearest-neighbor
For each crust point, write a Gaussian-weighted contribution into an accumulation buffer over a radius of roughly 2–3× point spacing, accumulating weight separately, then divide at the end.
for each point p:
  for each cell c within radius:
    w = exp(-d2 / (2 * sigma^2))
    field[c] += w * p.value
    wsum[c]  += w
field[c] /= wsum[c]
Cheap, parallel, smooth, and wraps trivially if the buffer is wrap-indexed. If moving to WebGL later, jump flooding gives the same result plus a distance field in a few passes.
Field ordering — important
Rasterize the raw fields (thickness, age, continental), then compute elevation from the rasterized fields. Not the reverse.
Thickness and age interpolate sensibly. Elevation is a nonlinear function of them, so interpolating elevation directly smears shelf breaks and ridge crests into mush.
The same pass gives you a distance-to-boundary field, used downstream for arc placement, wedge accretion falloff, and rift shoulder uplift.
