Fault symbology
Use real geologic symbols. It costs almost nothing and makes the map read instantly:
• Trenches — barbed teeth on the overriding side
• Ridges — double line with offset transform segments
• Normal faults / rifts — ticks on the hanging wall
• Strike-slip — paired arrows showing sense of motion
Obliquity colormap
For the raw (pre-segmentation) boundary network, use a 2D colormap: hue from obliquity, line width from rate. That single choice is most of the difference between a map that looks like a textbook diagram and one that looks like an actual tectonic map.
After ridge segmentation each piece is near-pure (≈0 or ≈π/2), so the segmented view simplifies — but keep the continuous obliquity value regardless, because oblique rifts and transpressional boundaries are real and should not be snapped to two categories.
Time controls
Play/pause, step-by-step, speed control, and a scrub bar over saved snapshots. Snapshot the full state every N steps so history can be replayed — this is essential for understanding why a feature formed, and you will want it constantly during tuning.
Cross-section view
Build this early. It is the primary debugging tool, not a later feature.
Why early
Slab dips will be wrong at first, and a map view will never reveal it. A cross-section shows a Wadati–Benioff zone that is either plausible or obviously broken in about one second. Same for crustal roots, thinning under rifts, and wedge geometry — all invisible from above, all immediately legible in section.
Implementation
Once there are two point layers this is nearly free:
1. User draws a line on the map (handle torus wrapping — the line may cross the seam).
2. Collect all crust and slab points within a corridor of half-width w (say 50–100 km) of the line.
3. Project each onto the line: s = distance along, and the point's depth.
4. Plot s on the horizontal axis, depth on the vertical, with vertical exaggeration as an adjustable control.
What to draw
• Crust as a band from elevation down to elevation − thickness, colored by continental fraction
• Mantle below, as background fill
• Slab points as their own dipping cloud, colored by age_at_sub or by coupling
• Sea level as a reference line
• Optional: the Moho as an explicit line at the base of the crust
