Validation targets
Check against these rather than against aesthetics. All are well-documented real-world cases.
Seafloor age map. Should look like the real one: symmetric stripes about ridges, offset across fracture zones, oldest crust adjacent to trenches. This is the single best diagnostic — if spreading and subduction are both right, it looks right immediately.
Ridge geometry. Orthogonal staircase. Fast ridges smooth with small offsets; slow ridges with large transform offsets.
Flat-slab. Should produce inland arc migration and basement uplift far from the trench (Laramide).
Rollback. Should produce back-arc extension, thinned crust, low elevation (Basin and Range).
Collision. Thick crustal root, high plateau, plate merge, and a persistent weakness lineament afterward.
Wilson cycle. Over 2 Gyr, continents gather and disperse, with new oceans preferentially opening along old sutures.
Reference reading for the implementer
• Cortial, Peytavie, Galin & Guérin (2019), Procedural Tectonic Planets, Computer Graphics Forum 38(2). Free PDF via LIRIS. Deliberately approximates subduction and collision procedurally rather than physically — the right tradeoff for this project. Note that it works on a sphere; adapt to the torus.
• Lauri Viitanen, platec — C++ library doing point-based 2D plate tectonics. Reading its source will save significant flailing on collision bookkeeping.
Notes on tuning
Expect most of the time to go into tuning, not implementation. Build the snapshot/replay system early, expose the constants as live sliders, and keep a seed so runs are reproducible. Parameters most worth exposing: L_seg, weakness decay half-life, rift trigger threshold, slab sink rate, wedge transfer fraction, and the isostatic DATUM.

Addendum — Review Revisions

An external review of this spec was incorporated. Changes below supersede the corresponding sections above.

Fix before implementation

Plate rotation → unwrapped coordinates. Rigid rotation is not globally well-defined on a flat torus. Give each plate an unwrapped local frame; each point carries (ux, uy) alongside its wrapped render position, and rotation is computed from the unwrapped offsets. Two cases the fix must handle: re-establish a point's unwrapped frame whenever it changes plate (subduction, rifting, suturing reassign ownership constantly), and force omega = 0 for any plate whose unwrapped extent exceeds W or H, where rotation is genuinely undefined.

Trench/hinge as an explicit kinematic state. Rollback must not be inferred by comparing sink rate to convergence rate. Add trench_position, trench_velocity, hinge_position, and rollback_rate to subduction-zone state. Rollback is then driven by slab negative buoyancy, dip, length, convergence, and trench anchoring — heuristically, no mantle model needed.

Ocean-depth flattening. The * 0 term in oceanDepth() is a bug. Replace with a blend that keeps sqrt behavior where it is valid and flattens smoothly:

js
const w = smoothstep(70, 110, t);
return (1 - w) * (2600 + 345 * Math.sqrt(t)) + w * DMAX;  // DMAX ≈ 5700–6000

Do not substitute a pure exponential — it is linear in age at small t and discards the sqrt relation, which is the well-established part. Relabel the section empirical age–bathymetry model, not half-space cooling.

Lithosphere as a distinct layer. Promoted from "recommended" to required — several processes in this spec (slab buoyancy, bending resistance, transform persistence, rollback, subduction initiation) are lithospheric, not crustal. Note that L ≈ k·sqrt(age) applies to oceanic lithosphere only; continental needs a separate rule, since cratonic roots exceed 200 km at ages in the billions. This also removes the hidden work currently done by the isostatic DATUM, and gives rifts a reason to route around old shields.

Ocean–ocean subduction polarity: probabilistic, with hysteresis. Score by age, boundary weakness, convergence, and a small random term rather than strict "older subducts." Critically: establish polarity once at subduction initiation and lock it, re-evaluating only on major reorganization. Re-scoring every step causes boundaries to flip between steps and corrupts the slab layer.

Mass conservation diagnostics. Track total crustal mass, total slab mass, and water volume; display conservation error. Accretionary wedge transfer must conserve mass explicitly.

Also adopted
Arc placement: treat ~100 km slab depth as a target with local variation, not a threshold test. Real arcs span roughly 65–130 km.
Triple junctions: detect as Voronoi vertices where three plate IDs meet; classify (RRR, RTF, TTT, etc.) and render. Nearly free, and the best available plate-topology debugger.
Earthquakes as diagnostic only: generate events from boundary stress × slip rate × weakness × fault type; render as dots. In cross-section this yields a Wadati–Benioff zone, which directly validates slab geometry.
Rifting driven by stress first, weakness second — not by continental area alone, which would make the largest continent rift simply for being large.
Segment length: modulate L_seg by inherited weakness and noise rather than a pure function of spreading rate. (Note: magma supply is not a state variable in this model; omit that term in v1.)
Slab identity: add slab_id and distance_along_slab so slab pull integrates over a coherent slab rather than summing independent points.
Rejected or modified
Segment-length/spreading-rate table stands as written. Fast-spreading ridges do have longer segments and smaller offsets; slow-spreading have shorter segments with large transform offsets. The review's heading claimed this was inverted; its own body text agrees with the table.
Staircase decomposition: wording softened only. The orthogonal staircase is the unique pure-mode decomposition; nature simply does not always adopt pure modes. This is a one-sentence clarification, not a change in approach.
Atlantic/Appalachian–Caledonian example retained as illustration. The algorithm was already general; no rule depended on the specific case.
Newly identified — not in either document

Point density management. Rifting stretches the point cloud and collision compresses it. Over a few hundred Myr, spacing drifts badly: gaps in extended crust, pileups in orogens. Requires point insertion and merging to maintain target spacing, while conserving mass. This is the most likely cause of a long run degrading quietly, and it is coupled to the mass diagnostics above.

Performance ceiling. Rebuilding Delaunay over 200k points per step in JS is likely the practical wall and was never costed. Benchmark before committing to the upper end of the point-count range; plan for incremental retriangulation if it fails.